@meta
id: 24-20
title: Scenario — wire up the consumer
chapter: 24
type: scenario
skills: ex-delete, ex-move, ex-copy, ex-join, ex-shift, buffer-switch
par: 81
minutes: 12
vscode: three tabs, an import sorter extension, and copy-paste into the docs
open: registry.ts order.consumer.ts CONSUMERS.md
vimrc: set hidden shiftwidth=2 expandtab

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    ORD-533  OrderConsumer is registered before the providers it needs
             and the docs still have an empty code block. Tidy the
             registry, strip the debug logging, document the event.

Three files, open as three buffers.

  1. registry.ts — the imports below the `container` line are unsorted
     and one is duplicated. Sort them, drop the duplicate, keep
     `container` first. The `OrderConsumer` registration must be the
     last registration in the file; the others keep their order.

  2. order.consumer.ts — the two `console.log` lines in
     `onOrderCreated` go. The two `await` lines that remain are one
     level too shallow. The `OrderCreatedEvent` type stays exactly as
     it is; you need a copy of it for the docs.

  3. CONSUMERS.md — the empty `ts` fence gets the `OrderCreatedEvent`
     type, all five lines, verbatim. Below it the queue URL has been
     wrapped in the middle of `order.created`; make it one line with
     nothing added at the break.

@keys
| `:2,9sort u` | sort lines 2 to 9, dropping duplicates |
| `:10m$` | move line 10 to the end of the file |
| `:4,8y a` | yank lines 4 to 8 into register `a` |
| `:15,16d` | delete lines 15 to 16 |
| `:15,16>` | indent lines 15 to 16 by one level |
| `:7pu a` | put register `a` below line 7 |
| `:15,16j!` | join lines 15 and 16 with nothing between |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer; write all and quit |

@hints
The registry first. After the sort and dedupe the file is one line
shorter, so read the gutter again before moving the consumer
registration.
--
In the consumer, yank the type into a named register before you do
anything else, and go straight from there to the docs when the file is
done. `:pu` takes a line number, so you do not need to find the fence
with the cursor.
--
After the put, the wrapped URL has moved down five lines. `j!`, not
`j`: a space inside a URL is a bug.
--
Par assumes no cursor movement at all, in any of the three files.

@solution
A route that measures seventy keystrokes.

**registry.ts** — `:2,9sort u`. The imports are now lines 2 to 8 and
the registrations start on line 10, with `OrderConsumer` first:
`:10m$`.

**order.consumer.ts** — `:bn`. `:4,8y a` takes the type. `:15,16d`
drops the logging; the two `await` lines slide up into 15 and 16, so
`:15,16>` indents them.

**CONSUMERS.md** — `:bn`. `:7pu a` drops the type inside the fence. The
URL is now on lines 15 and 16: `:15,16j!`. `:wqa`.

This is the whole chapter in one ticket. Delete, move, copy, join and
shift are one verb each, every one of them takes the same range in
front, and the register form of yank and put is what carries text
between files when `m` and `t` cannot. The cursor stayed on line 1 of
every file, because it had no reason to be anywhere else.

Two things worth noticing. First, the yank happened before the delete,
and the put used a line number, so nothing depended on where the
cursor had ended up. Second, every line number was read off the screen
at the moment it was needed, not planned in advance. Both are the habits
that make the ex language reliable rather than clever.

@start registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderConsumer } from '../domain/order/order.consumer';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { EmailService } from '../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderConsumer', { useClass: OrderConsumer });
container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
container.register('EmailService', { useClass: EmailService });

@expected registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { EmailService } from '../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { OrderConsumer } from '../domain/order/order.consumer';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
container.register('EmailService', { useClass: EmailService });
container.register('OrderConsumer', { useClass: OrderConsumer });

@start order.consumer.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

type OrderCreatedEvent = {
  orderId: string;
  customerId: string;
  total: number;
};

@injectable()
export class OrderConsumer {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async onOrderCreated(event: OrderCreatedEvent) {
  console.log('order.created', event);
  console.log(JSON.stringify(event));
  await this.orderProvider.sendReceipt(event.orderId);
  await this.orderProvider.notifyWarehouse(event.orderId);
  }
}

@expected order.consumer.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

type OrderCreatedEvent = {
  orderId: string;
  customerId: string;
  total: number;
};

@injectable()
export class OrderConsumer {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async onOrderCreated(event: OrderCreatedEvent) {
    await this.orderProvider.sendReceipt(event.orderId);
    await this.orderProvider.notifyWarehouse(event.orderId);
  }
}

